{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794844000,"nonce":"30HVDQIF9OrgKisdXzfbXNVZrhw=","message":"77650d056b7dfcf293b51a2b277bc251ee42cfb5: nAIR programs run on the real engines. Nine of the ten bench programs (all but DeltaBlue, which still stops in haxe.Log.formatOutput on Array\u003cDynamic\u003e) are lowered whole from the init function, written as a .hl by ash_nair_hl (types, globals, string constants, stubs for methods a method table names that nothing lowered defines) and run beside genhl's file on jit and hybrid. With --no-fma every one prints exactly what genhl's file prints (Fib, FieldAccess, MethodCall, ArrayAccess, BinaryTrees, NBody, SpectralNorm, Fannkuch, Mandelbrot), exit codes equal. Without it Mandelbrot differs, 112798515 (nAIR) against 112798587 (genhl): the peephole fuses the multiply-adds of the two bytecode shapes differently; 112798515 is what clang's default contraction gives, which the memory of ground truths records as hxcpp/hxjava's value. Timing is level on most (NBody .68 s both, SpectralNorm .60 against .68, Fannkuch .38 against .40 on jit) but BinaryTrees is slower from nAIR's file (jit .48 s against .21 s, hybrid .23 against .06), which is the next thing to look at: the allocation path, or the extra functions. These are single runs on a loaded Mac; confirm on the NUC before believing a timing.","files":null}]}